config ZMK_RGBEFFECT
    bool "ZMK RGB Player custom module"
    default y
    help
      Enables the zmk-rgb-player module: per-pixel LED control, RGB
      effects, score playback engine, custom BLE GATT service, USB
      CDC debug console, and DFU trigger.

if ZMK_RGBEFFECT

config ZMK_RGB_PLAYER_LOG_LEVEL
    int "ZMK RGB Player log level"
    default 4
    range 0 4
    help
      Log level for the zmk-rgb-player module. 0=off, 1=err, 2=wrn,
      3=inf, 4=dbg. Used by LOG_MODULE_DECLARE/REGISTER macros.

config ZMK_RGB_PLAYER_BRINGUP
    bool "Hardware bring-up self test (light the strip without any key press)"
    default n
    help
      Diagnostic build for a freshly soldered board. At boot the strip runs a
      chain self test (fill -> walking pixel -> all-on), then every key press
      lights that key's own pixel, and the whole strip blinks once every 5 s as
      a heartbeat. This makes the LEDs observable on a board whose matrix does
      not work yet, which the normal firmware cannot do because it keeps the
      active effect OFF and only a key press can light a pixel.

      While enabled, effect switching is inert and the strip has exactly one
      writer (src/bringup.c). Turn this off once the hardware is verified.

config ZMK_RGB_PLAYER_DEFAULT_EFFECT
    int "RGB effect active at boot"
    default 3
    range 0 6
    help
      Which effect the strip shows after boot. 0=OFF, 1=solid single colour,
      2=breathing, 3=rainbow, 4=single-key, 5=twinkle, 6=ripple.

      The 4-corner combo (Y+N+P+/) cycles through all seven at runtime, so this
      only decides what you see before you ever press that combo. It matters
      because the firmware default is 0 (OFF) and effects_init() does not start
      the render tick on its own - without this setting the strip stays dark at
      boot and that looks exactly like "the LEDs are broken".

      Cost note: 0 (OFF) and 1 (solid) are near-free; the animated effects
      re-render the strip on a tick (see effects.c), which is the single
      largest optional current draw in this firmware. Set 0 for the longest
      battery life.

config ZMK_RGB_PLAYER_BTDIAG
    bool "BLE link-latency probe on the blue LED"
    default n
    help
      TEMPORARY diagnostic. Measures the connection parameters the host
      actually granted - interval AND slave latency - and blinks the on-board
      blue LED 1..5 times per key press, where the count encodes the worst-case
      wait for a keystroke in ms (1: <=10, 2: <=20, 3: <=50, 4: <=100,
      5: >100). A separate nine-blink boot signature identifies the build.

      It exists because ZMK only advertises a connection-parameter PREFERENCE
      and never asks the host to honour it (app/src/ble.c has no
      bt_conn_le_param_update call at all), so "is the link slow because the
      host relaxed it while idle" cannot be answered from Kconfig. It has to be
      measured.

      Turn this off once the question is answered: it costs a thread, a
      connection callback and a key-press subscription.

endif # ZMK_RGBEFFECT
